---
import Comparison from '../../components/Comparison.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { link } from '../../utils/base';
---

<Base
  title="Field Notes compared with tldraw and Excalidraw"
  description="Licence, framework, bundle size, sync, export, layers and frames in Field Notes, tldraw and Excalidraw, each claim with a source and the date it was checked."
>
  <main class="compare">
    <div class="wrap head">
      <h1 id="compare-title">Field Notes compared with tldraw and Excalidraw</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Each claim about tldraw or Excalidraw has a numbered source and the date it was checked. A
        blank cell means no first-party source was found for it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Comparison rows="all" />

    <div class="wrap choices">
      <section aria-labelledby="tldraw">
        <h2 id="tldraw">When tldraw is the better choice</h2>
        <p>
          tldraw ships a <a href="https://tldraw.dev/docs/user-interface">complete editor interface</a
          >: toolbars, menus and style panels. Field Notes has no tool toolbar or style panel, so an
          app that needs that interface on day one, and can take the <a
            href="https://tldraw.dev/community/license">production licence</a
          >, gets there sooner with tldraw. Its text editor is <a
            href="https://tldraw.dev/sdk-features/rich-text">built on TipTap</a
          >, with lists and links. Its repository was <a
            href="https://api.github.com/repos/tldraw/tldraw">created in 2021</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="excalidraw">
        <h2 id="excalidraw">When Excalidraw is the better choice</h2>
        <p>
          Excalidraw is under the <a
            href="https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw/blob/master/LICENSE">MIT licence</a
          > too, and its React component <a
            href="https://plus.excalidraw.com/docs/sdk/excalidraw/api/children-components/main-menu"
            >renders its own menus</a
          >. For a React app that wants that whiteboard as it is, one component does it. Its
          repository is the <a href="https://api.github.com/repos/excalidraw/excalidraw"
            >oldest and most starred</a
          > of the three.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="fits">
        <h2 id="fits">When Field Notes fits</h2>
        <p>
          The app isn't a React app, or it wants the board without an editor interface on top: <a
            href={link('/docs/getting-started/')}><code>@fieldnotes/core</code></a
          > is vanilla TypeScript with no runtime dependencies. The licence is MIT, with no key and no
          watermark. The board holds <a href={link('/docs/html-elements/')}>live HTML elements</a>, <a
            href={link('/docs/layers/')}>named layers</a
          > and <a
            href={link('/docs/tools-and-input/#frames')}>frames</a
          >, <a href={link('/docs/export/')}>exports to PDF</a>, and has a <a
            href={link('/docs/tabletop-maps/')}>tabletop package</a
          > with grids and fog of war. The <a href={link('/docs/multiplayer/')}>sync server</a> is a
          WebSocket relay you run yourself.
        </p>
      </section>

      <p class="closing">
        What Field Notes doesn't do yet is on <a href={link('/roadmap/#not-built')}>the roadmap</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .compare {
    padding-top: 48px;
    padding-bottom: 96px;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 44px;
    line-height: 1.1;
  }

  .intro {
    max-width: 36em;
    margin: 16px 0 0;
    color: var(--graphite-2);
    font-size: 19px;
  }

  .choices {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 40px;
    margin-top: 64px;
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 28px;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  section p,
  .closing {
    max-width: 40em;
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  code {
    font-size: 15px;
  }
</style>
